{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RbGtdKioTazFGczVuGFrop","type":"tool_result","content":"44:The hardest and most practically relevant case is a new model that is \\emph{better than all sources}: its ability lies outside the range spanned by the historical matrix, so any estimator that is tuned to source models must extrapolate. A method that works for models resembling the sources may degrade exactly where evaluation savings matter most, namely when comparing new, stronger systems. Predicting the behaviour of more capable models from results on weaker ones is a recognized and difficult target elsewhere in language-model evaluation---observational scaling laws, for example, fit capa\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix; with a vector of abilities per model and item-specific loadings it is a compensatory multidimensional IRT model, whose item information and ability estimation are treated in \\citet{Reckase2009MultidimensionalIR}, and the item-information criterion we use for selection is the same quantity that drives item selection in adaptive testing \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE}. The final estimator follows the model-assisted approach of survey samplin\n64:\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix; with a vector of abilities per model and item-specific loadings it is a compensatory multidimensional IRT model, whose item information and ability estimation are treated in \\citet{Reckase2009MultidimensionalIR}, and the item-information criterion we use for selection is the same quantity that drives item selection in adaptive testing \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE}. The final estimator follows the model-assisted approach of survey samplin\n\n## Repair round 2 (2026-11-11)\n- Audit fix: removed